cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "PQC certificate compatibility matrix"
message: >-
  If you use this measurement corpus or cite its findings, please cite it as
  below. Check PRIOR-ART.md first: it records, per finding, what published work
  already covers, and several claims here are refinements of prior results
  rather than new ones.
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: "Danny B."
    family-names: Carr
    name-suffix: Jr.
    affiliation: "Carr Digital LLC"
repository-code: "https://github.com/DannyBCarrJr/pqc-cert-matrix"
url: "https://carrdigital.dev/writing/hybrid-certificates-weighed/"
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.21749600
identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.21749600
    description: >-
      Concept DOI. Always resolves to the latest version; cite this one unless
      you need to pin a specific release.
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.21749601
    description: Version DOI for v1.0.0.
abstract: >-
  Measured client behaviour for post-quantum and hybrid X.509 certificate
  chains. Eight chain shapes (ECDSA control, ML-DSA-44/65/87, an SLH-DSA root
  variant, a mixed classical-over-post-quantum chain, a catalyst-style hybrid
  carrying alternative-signature extensions, and a composite signature
  certificate) tested against eleven client stacks: OpenSSL 3.0 and 3.5, GnuTLS,
  Go, Java 21, .NET 8, Python 3.13, Node 22, rustls, Bouncy Castle, and Windows
  schannel. Each of the 88 cells records certificate parsing, offline chain
  validation, and a live TLS 1.3 handshake, backed by a script and captured
  output. Also includes transport measurements from key-log-decrypted
  handshakes: per-message byte attribution, TLS record structure, certificate
  compression effectiveness, and a congestion window analysis.
keywords:
  - post-quantum cryptography
  - X.509
  - TLS
  - public key infrastructure
  - ML-DSA
  - hybrid certificates
  - interoperability
license: MIT
version: 1.0.0
date-released: "2026-08-01"
